---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL, apis, meta } from "../lib/data";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const s = meta.score;
---

<Base title="Methodology: API Weather" description="How API Weather samples spec history, counts breaking changes and computes the stability score.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How this works</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Each vendor publishes an OpenAPI description of their API in a public GitHub repo. Its git history is a record of every
      change to the contract. API Weather reads that history, compares one month to the next, and counts what would break a
      client.
    </p>

    <h2>Which specs</h2>
    <p>
      {apis.length} APIs, each tracked through a single spec file in the vendor's own repository. The list, with file paths, is
      in <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/apis.json`}><code>apis.json</code></a>. Each API's page links to its exact file.
    </p>

    <h2>Sampling</h2>
    <p>
      For every month since the file first appeared, the pipeline takes the spec as it stood on the default branch at the end of
      that month. Months where it didn't change are skipped. The current month is a running snapshot and is replaced on every
      daily run.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a month's spec is missing or isn't OpenAPI 3, the timeline shows a hatched gap and nothing across it is counted. OpenAI's
      spec, for example, disappeared from its repo for most of a year.
    </p>

    <h2>What counts as breaking</h2>
    <p>
      Consecutive snapshots are compared with <a href="https://github.com/oasdiff/oasdiff">oasdiff</a>
      ({meta.oasdiff.replace("oasdiff version ", "")}), using its own breaking-change rules with one adjustment:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Breaking</strong> is anything oasdiff rates as an error: a removed endpoint or field, a changed type, a request
        field that became required, a response field that can now be null.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Soft</strong> covers oasdiff's warnings plus additive response changes, such as a new enum value or a new
        <code>oneOf</code> branch. Strictly generated clients can fail on these, but most vendors document them as safe, so they
        count for a quarter.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      oasdiff reports a schema change once for every endpoint and nesting path that uses it. A single new Stripe enum value can
      produce 7,000 findings. They're grouped back into one change, with a count of the endpoints it touches. Every number on this
      site is a grouped count.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vendors that ship preview endpoints usually warn that those may break. When every endpoint a breaking change touches is
      marked beta or unstable in the spec, it's listed on the API's page but left out of the totals and the score. The markers
      read are Datadog's <code>x-unstable</code>, Cloudflare's <code>x-stability: beta</code>, Box's
      <code>x-stability-level</code>, Okta's early-access <code>x-okta-lifecycle</code>, Sentry's
      <code>x-sentry-experimental</code> and GitHub's <code>x-github.previews</code>. The other APIs don't mark preview
      endpoints in their specs, so all of their changes count.
    </p>

    <h2>The stability score</h2>
    <p>For each of the last {s.windowMonths} months, the penalty is breaking changes plus soft changes at {s.softWeight} weight, per 100 endpoints:</p>
    <pre><code>penalty(month) = (breaking + {s.softWeight} × soft) / (endpoints / 100)
pressure       = Σ penalty(month) × 0.5 ^ (months ago / {s.halfLifeMonths})
score          = 100 × e ^ (−pressure / {s.k})</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>Dividing by endpoints keeps a large API from looking worse just for being large.</li>
      <li>Recent months weigh more, halving every {s.halfLifeMonths} months.</li>
      <li>
        The constant {s.k} was set once, after the first full run, so a typical API lands near 70. It stays fixed so scores can be
        compared over time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>What this doesn't tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Versioned APIs.</strong> Stripe, GitHub and Plaid version their APIs. A breaking change to the latest contract
        doesn't reach clients pinned to an older version until they upgrade.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The spec isn't the API.</strong> When a vendor corrects an inaccurate spec, it shows up here as a change even if the
        live API never moved.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Broken specs.</strong> Some specs reference schemas that don't exist. Those references are replaced with empty
        schemas so oasdiff can read the file, which hides changes inside them. Each API page says how often that happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Within-month churn.</strong> Something added and removed in the same month never shows up.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reproduce it</h2>
    <p>
      The pipeline, raw data and this site are in <a href={REPO_URL}>one repository</a>. A GitHub Action re-runs the pipeline
      daily and commits the results. See the <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/data/README.md`}>data README</a> for file formats.
    </p>
    <p><a href={`${base}/`}>Back to the report</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    padding-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    max-width: 44rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, var(--t5));
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--t1);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--t2);
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  pre {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--wash);
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  pre code {
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
  }
</style>
